1
00:00:00,256 --> 00:00:06,153
You have a woodland worth keeping. Let's take it out of the
editor, bring it back as a separate save,

2
00:00:06,187 --> 00:00:10,170
and check that it still works. Then we'll export one piece of the
scene,

3
00:00:10,204 --> 00:00:14,086
so you can see the difference between a whole project and a
reusable asset.

4
00:00:19,771 --> 00:00:25,930
We'll make a lesson copy, export and import the whole save, and
check a few objects in the imported version.

5
00:00:26,054 --> 00:00:31,909
After a short play test, we'll export one Stone Road and transfer
it into an empty sandbox.

6
00:00:32,113 --> 00:00:35,000
Keep your original woodland available throughout this lesson.

7
00:00:35,642 --> 00:00:39,683
Start in the finished woodland. Open the System Menu and Quick
Save.

8
00:00:39,738 --> 00:00:44,974
Wait for the saved confirmation before leaving. Quick Save stores
the current editing save.

9
00:00:45,029 --> 00:00:48,025
It doesn't give you a separate file in a folder on your computer.

10
00:00:48,101 --> 00:00:51,316
For that, we need the export operation on the save page.

11
00:00:52,086 --> 00:00:54,621
Return to the save page from the editor's menu.

12
00:00:54,646 --> 00:01:00,138
If you're starting from the normal game instead, open the Paimon
Menu and My Miliastra Wonderland.

13
00:01:00,278 --> 00:01:04,514
This page lists your editing saves. Select the woodland card
carefully,

14
00:01:04,568 --> 00:01:08,631
because the card you choose determines which project the next
operation uses.

15
00:01:09,489 --> 00:01:14,180
Open the card's menu and choose Copy Save. Give the copy a useful
lesson name.

16
00:01:14,286 --> 00:01:19,864
Here, it's Woodland Workshop, episode three. The earlier lesson
remains on its own card.

17
00:01:19,918 --> 00:01:24,322
A separate copy gives us somewhere to practise without changing
the version we just finished.

18
00:01:24,484 --> 00:01:26,404
Check the new card before continuing.

19
00:01:36,438 --> 00:01:41,120
On the lesson copy, open the card menu again and choose Export
Save File.

20
00:01:44,872 --> 00:01:49,959
A file dialog opens. Choose your own project folder and enter a
recognisable file name.

21
00:01:49,992 --> 00:01:52,908
The whole-save file ends in dot G I L.

22
00:01:53,032 --> 00:01:57,038
I'll show the relevant parts of the dialog enlarged, so you can
read them.

23
00:01:57,766 --> 00:02:01,466
The name on the editor card and the name of the exported file are
separate.

24
00:02:01,563 --> 00:02:07,788
Changing one doesn't rename the other. I use Woodland, episode
three, Tutorial for this export.

25
00:02:07,906 --> 00:02:12,760
You can use your own name. Keep the file extension and choose a
folder you'll be able to find again.

26
00:02:13,494 --> 00:02:17,663
Think of this file as a copy of this editing project at the moment
it was exported.

27
00:02:17,761 --> 00:02:19,882
It isn't a live connection to the card.

28
00:02:19,979 --> 00:02:24,588
If you change the woodland later, export again when you want an
updated external copy.

29
00:02:24,656 --> 00:02:28,513
Keep an earlier file separately if you want to preserve that
earlier state.

30
00:02:29,289 --> 00:02:32,511
Back on the save page, choose Import Save.

31
00:02:33,183 --> 00:02:37,791
Find the dot G I L file you just exported, select it, and open it.

32
00:02:38,428 --> 00:02:44,027
Wait for the new card to appear. Check that the source lesson and
the imported card are both still available.

33
00:02:44,052 --> 00:02:46,698
We're going to open the imported one for the next checks.

34
00:02:47,370 --> 00:02:51,539
Our imported card is called Woodland, episode three, Tutorial.

35
00:02:51,722 --> 00:02:55,367
Before editing anything, check its title and recognise the scene.

36
00:02:55,496 --> 00:03:00,156
A familiar thumbnail alone is easy to misread when several copies
look the same.

37
00:03:00,318 --> 00:03:05,666
Keep track of the source card, the external file, and the imported
card as three separate things.

38
00:03:06,453 --> 00:03:10,239
Select a Stone Road group and read its name and Transform values.

39
00:03:10,379 --> 00:03:15,843
Then look at the tree, rock, and Rain Pavilion. We're checking
that different parts of the arrangement

40
00:03:15,868 --> 00:03:21,424
came across, rather than assuming that one visible object proves
the entire save is correct.

41
00:03:21,542 --> 00:03:24,888
Your own scene may use different models or coordinates.

42
00:03:31,455 --> 00:03:36,435
Quick Save the imported version and start a private Test Play.
Wait for the character to appear.

43
00:03:36,618 --> 00:03:42,141
Look towards the pavilion, walk a short distance along the central
stones, and release the movement key.

44
00:03:42,226 --> 00:03:46,438
In this check, the route is visible and the character moves along
the ground.

45
00:03:46,664 --> 00:03:49,944
Test your own important interactions when your project has them.

46
00:04:06,432 --> 00:04:11,379
Leave the private test, return to the imported editing save, and
save any changes.

47
00:04:11,528 --> 00:04:15,782
This short test checks the restored scene and a small part of its
walking route.

48
00:04:15,880 --> 00:04:17,873
It doesn't check every possible action.

49
00:04:17,970 --> 00:04:23,776
Later, when the challenge has coins and a timer, those systems
will need their own checks after import.

50
00:04:29,440 --> 00:04:34,847
Now we have a whole woodland again. What if we only want one set
of stones for another project?

51
00:04:35,029 --> 00:04:41,404
Exporting the entire save would carry much more than we need.
Asset export makes a different kind of file,

52
00:04:41,429 --> 00:04:46,592
ending in dot G I A. We'll try it with one selected Stone Road
entity.

53
00:04:47,285 --> 00:04:50,388
Select the Stone Road in the scene and check its name on the
right.

54
00:04:50,442 --> 00:04:54,282
Right-click the selected entity, then choose Export Entity.

55
00:05:02,271 --> 00:05:07,163
Choose a file name for this asset and save it. The selection
matters here: this example

56
00:05:07,188 --> 00:05:12,569
exports one placed Stone Road group, with its several visible
stones, as one entity.

57
00:05:13,419 --> 00:05:18,642
The official guide also explains that an exported entity can carry
its associated prefab and

58
00:05:18,667 --> 00:05:24,009
mounted node graphs. When several assets refer to each other,
exporting the related content

59
00:05:24,128 --> 00:05:29,335
together helps preserve their relationships. Our scenery example
is deliberately small.

60
00:05:29,432 --> 00:05:33,758
We haven't built or checked a package of linked gameplay systems
in this lesson.

61
00:05:34,599 --> 00:05:38,747
For the import demonstration, open a separate blank lesson save.

62
00:05:38,908 --> 00:05:43,505
I call this the Asset Sandbox. Keep the restored woodland
untouched.

63
00:05:43,562 --> 00:05:48,435
An empty scene makes the result easy to recognise: if the asset
transfer works,

64
00:05:48,511 --> 00:05:53,874
we should get the selected Stone Road, rather than the trees,
pavilion, and the rest of the route.

65
00:05:54,564 --> 00:05:58,477
Open the System Menu, then Manage Asset Import and Export.

66
00:05:58,576 --> 00:06:04,152
Switch to Import and Transfer, and choose Load External Assets.
Follow the load prompt.

67
00:06:04,208 --> 00:06:09,356
Our exported Stone Road appears in the list. Loading an asset
makes it available in this tool;

68
00:06:09,390 --> 00:06:12,614
we still need to select and transfer it into the current save.

69
00:06:13,318 --> 00:06:19,224
Select the Stone Road and choose Transfer Selected Assets. Read
the confirmation before accepting it.

70
00:06:19,309 --> 00:06:24,489
In this version, object entities go directly into the current
save, and the transfer clears

71
00:06:24,514 --> 00:06:29,950
its undo and redo history. That's why we're demonstrating the
operation in the empty sandbox.

72
00:06:30,076 --> 00:06:33,101
Check the current save and selection before you confirm.

73
00:06:36,332 --> 00:06:40,586
After the transfer, one Stone Road group appears in the empty
scene.

74
00:06:40,726 --> 00:06:46,255
Select it and read back its name and Transform. There are several
individual stones in the model,

75
00:06:46,289 --> 00:06:51,354
but this selection is one entity. The woodland's trees and
pavilion aren't here.

76
00:06:51,494 --> 00:06:55,494
That visible difference tells us what this particular asset file
contained.

77
00:06:56,302 --> 00:07:01,141
Quick Save the sandbox and wait for the confirmation. Keep the
asset file as well if you

78
00:07:01,166 --> 00:07:06,160
want to reuse it again. The current sandbox and the external asset
are separate copies.

79
00:07:06,272 --> 00:07:10,790
Editing the placed stones here won't automatically update the file
you exported earlier.

80
00:07:11,684 --> 00:07:15,612
For your own check, find the whole-save file and import it
separately.

81
00:07:15,679 --> 00:07:21,372
Open the new card, recognise the scene, inspect a few objects, and
try the important route.

82
00:07:21,398 --> 00:07:26,280
Then identify the single-entity asset and explain what it should
add to an empty save.

83
00:07:26,452 --> 00:07:30,172
Being able to find and verify these copies is the useful skill.

84
00:07:31,023 --> 00:07:35,641
Keep your lesson saves and external files clearly named as the
series grows.

85
00:07:35,759 --> 00:07:40,112
You don't need to copy my exact folder names. You do need to know
which file is

86
00:07:40,137 --> 00:07:44,733
the whole project, which is the reusable asset, and which version
you tested.

87
00:07:44,787 --> 00:07:48,588
The companion guide includes the menu paths and the official
references.

88
00:07:49,340 --> 00:07:55,216
In the next lesson, we'll return to a copy of the complete
woodland and make a collectible with native nodes.

89
00:07:55,314 --> 00:07:58,691
Walking into it should count once and remove that coin.

90
00:07:58,849 --> 00:08:03,763
We'll check that small interaction before adding the progress bar
and the timed challenge.
